Concerns/Problems/Discoveries: My create_key function wouldn't output the correct
key no matter how many times I would try to fix it and run through it with
gdb. I had to remember that it matters what registers you use because the register
I was using to store the key wasn't callee-saved. There were also nuances in the
actual logic of my assembly code like having to make sure the key returned
didn't have garbage values in it by clearing the upper bits of the register that 
stored the key. Also, I had to make sure to use the 1 byte version of 
instructions to only affect what was currently being read in. 

gdb usage: I didn't use gdb to find a bug in the C language program.

gdb usage for assembly: I used gdb to find the bug in my create_key function and
the small error in my rotate_left function. I set breakpoints in main, create_key,
and the rotate functions to see what was going wrong. I used the "tui reg general"
command to look at all the values in the registers at each line in the assembly
functions. I determined the bug in my create_key function because I realized
that the %edx register was not saving the key after calling getchar because I 
wasn't properly using callee-saved and caller-saved registers. I also used gdb
to fix the bug in my rotate_left function by seeing that I had not properly
set up my stack frame at the very beginning of the assembly code. I compared the 
register values to the rotate_right function because I had set that one up
properly and realized my error after checking back on slide decks in class as well.

Jumping between C/x86-64 programs: Yes, I did jump between my C program and my x86-64
program to fix some bugs. I had forgotten the logic on how to rotate the bits 
so I referred back to my C code in project 2 to refresh my memory to write the
assembly code for this project. The code did turn out slightly different because I 
used two registers to store the rotated value instead of doing the exact logic in C
where I shifted left/right first, had the helper value, and then combined the results
together.
